#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-spend — the box's one token ledger: what every claude process spent, and which of them nobody is watching.

WHY. Spend was only ever visible per run (`runs/*.json`) or per session (`/cost`), so a worker left looping in a
worktree nobody had a window on burned usage invisibly. This reads the one thing that records every call — the
transcripts under ~/.claude/projects — so it needs no model, no network and no cooperation from what it measures.

THE TICK (`cc-spend tick`, every 5 min from cc-spend.timer). Every *.jsonl under ~/.claude/projects, at any depth
— a subagent writes its own, one level deeper under <session>/subagents/, carrying the parent's sessionId. Each
transcript is read only from the byte offset the last tick left in ~/.cc/state/spend/offsets.json, keyed by full
path; a file that shrank or whose inode changed is re-read from 0, one that vanished is forgotten. Assistant
records fold to one row per (session, model, UTC day), appended to ~/.cc/state/spend/<YYYY-MM-DD>.tsv, the day
in the name being UTC's. Rows are dated by the RECORD's timestamp, not the tick's, so the first tick backfills
every day this box already has instead of piling months of history onto today.

THE ONE TRAP. A single API response is written as MANY assistant records — one per content block, 15 seen on a
long turn — and every one carries the FULL usage. Summing records triples the bill. Rows dedupe on requestId, and
offsets.json remembers the last requestId of the previous read, so a response split across a tick boundary is
counted once and not twice. The same guard spans FILES: a tick dedupes (session, requestId) across every
transcript it reads, and a sidechain record in a parent transcript is dropped only when the persisted registry
of subagent requestIds (offsets.json's sub_rids) shows a subagent file really carries that same requestId —
otherwise it is counted, because nothing else ever will be.

TOKENS ARE THE TRUTH, USD IS AN ESTIMATE — the CLI says so itself. There is ONE rates table (below), fitted
against every runs/*.json this box has: it reproduces Claude Code's own modelUsage costUSD to the cent.

PHANTOMS. Each tick also snapshots the live `claude` processes to procs.tsv. A phantom is spend nobody is
watching: a session whose transcript grew this tick with no tmux window on its cwd and no live board row behind
it, or a process alive over an hour whose transcript has not grown in thirty minutes. A window is ON a cwd when
its pane sits there or deeper — never a parent, or the `box` window parked at ~/dev would watch the whole box.
The tick WRITES that list to phantoms.tsv as well as printing it on demand, so a reader (the Slack Home tab) can
show it without re-deriving the rule from tmux, the boards and the loops — and the answer it shows is the same
one `cc-spend phantoms` gives, because `grew` only changes at a tick anyway.

  cc-spend                     today, by session, biggest first
  cc-spend --by repo|track|model|kind [--week]
  cc-spend --week              the last 7 days
  cc-spend today [<repo>]      what AUTONOMOUS work has booked today, per repo; with a repo, that one number
  cc-spend cache [--days N]    cache-read tokens per session per day, biggest first (default 7 days)
  cc-spend --json              today, the week and the last 7 days one by one, for the dashboard
  cc-spend phantoms            spend with nobody watching it (the tick also saves it to phantoms.tsv)
  cc-spend tick                what the timer runs
  cc-spend selfcheck           fixtures in a HOME of its own — no network, never this box's ledger

TWO DAYS, AND WHICH IS WHICH. The LEDGER is UTC: a row keeps its UTC timestamp and lands in the UTC-named day
file, because that is what reconciliation compares against the logs, the journals and the boards, and mixing
the two boundaries is where reconciliation has broken before. Every VIEW above — the default, `--week`,
`today`, `cache`, `--json` — buckets by the day the BOX is in, because that is what the owner means by "today".
The zone is the system's (`$TZ`, else /etc/localtime, else /etc/timezone) and never a config this repo owns, so
moving the box is `timedatectl set-timezone` and no edit here. Each view names the zone it used in its header;
`--json` carries it as `tz` with `day_boundary: "local"`. Bucketing converts each row's INSTANT and reads the
date off it, so a 23- or 25-hour DST day is right without anyone special-casing it.

THE DASHBOARD'S SHAPE (`--json`, agreed 2026-09-07). `tz` the IANA name, `day_boundary` which boundary produced
the figures — a fact to label with, not a version to infer from — and `today`, `week`, `by_day` bucketed by the
box-local day, `by_day` keyed on local dates. Changing those five names means telling the dashboard track first.

TODAY, PER REPO (`cc-spend today`). A daily ceiling has one question: what has AUTONOMOUS work — every `claude
-p`, so worker loops and landing rounds, never a person at a terminal — booked on this repo today. That is a
read of the ledger TSVs the box's own today spans and nothing else; a second collector would be a second
truth. Two things any caller must know. It counts what was SPENT, where a member workspace's cap (cc: member_gate) PRE-charges what a dispatch
MAY spend — the two never agree, and only this one lags. They no longer roll together either: this figure rolls
at the box's local midnight and that cap still rolls at UTC's, so for the hours between them a repo's ceiling
and its spend are counting different days. And it is only as current as
the last tick: when the ledger has stopped moving the number is still printed, but stderr says so and the exit
status is 1, because a cap that reads a frozen ledger as headroom is the silent failure this exists to prevent.
The ceiling itself is the owner's number and is not here: this answers the question, for him at a terminal or
for whatever asks it.
"""
import fcntl
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import time
from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone
from zoneinfo import ZoneInfo

H = os.path.expanduser("~")
PROJ = os.path.join(H, ".claude", "projects")
ST = os.path.join(H, ".cc", "state", "spend")
OFF = os.path.join(ST, "offsets.json")
PROCS = os.path.join(ST, "procs.tsv")
PHANTOMS = os.path.join(ST, "phantoms.tsv")
WTS = os.path.join(H, ".cc", "worktrees")
DEV = os.path.join(H, "dev")
STEWARD = os.path.join(H, ".cc", "steward")
BOARDS = os.path.join(H, ".cc", "boards")
STATE = os.path.join(H, ".cc", "state")
SCRATCH = "cc-land"        # /tmp/cc-land.run.<pid>.<random>/cc-land.<step>.<repo>.<pr>.<random> — the throwaway
SCRATCH_LANE = "landing"   # checkout a review or a gate round runs in, under the run's own root; see place(). The
                           # lane an older, unparseable one falls back to.

COLS = ["ts", "session", "cwd", "repo", "track", "kind", "model", "in", "out", "cache_read", "cache_write", "est_usd"]
PCOLS = ["ts", "pid", "ppid", "etime_s", "cwd", "repo", "track", "kind", "model", "session"]
PHCOLS = ["ts", "why", "what", "repo", "track", "kind", "detail", "cwd"]

# ONE rates table, $ per MILLION tokens: (input, output, cache read). Cache WRITES are 1.25x input at the 5-minute
# TTL and 2x at the 1-hour one, and `usage.cache_creation` says which of the two a write was — so a write is
# priced, never guessed. Claude Fable 5.1's cache read is 0.025x input rather than the usual 0.1x. These numbers
# were fitted against every ~/.cc/state/*/*/runs/*.json on this box and reproduce its modelUsage costUSD exactly.
# A model with no row here is still counted in tokens; its est_usd is 0 and the views say how many rows that was.
RATES = {
    "claude-fable-5-1":  (10.0, 50.0, 0.25),
    "claude-mythos-5-1": (10.0, 50.0, 0.25),
    "claude-fable-5":    (10.0, 50.0, 1.00),
    "claude-mythos-5":   (10.0, 50.0, 1.00),
    "claude-opus-5":     (5.0, 25.0, 0.50),
    "claude-opus-4-8":   (5.0, 25.0, 0.50),
    "claude-opus-4-7":   (5.0, 25.0, 0.50),
    "claude-opus-4-6":   (5.0, 25.0, 0.50),
    "claude-sonnet-5":   (2.0, 10.0, 0.20),
    "claude-sonnet-4-6": (3.0, 15.0, 0.30),
    "claude-haiku-4-5":  (1.0, 5.0, 0.10),
}
# Fast mode is the same model at premium prices (Opus only) — `usage.speed` names it, so it is not a guess either.
FAST = {"claude-opus-5": (10.0, 50.0, 0.50), "claude-opus-4-8": (10.0, 50.0, 0.50)}
W_5M, W_1H = 1.25, 2.0            # the cache-write multiples on the input rate, by TTL

TICK_PERIOD = 300                 # cc-spend.timer's period: the ledger cannot be fresher than one tick
LEDGER_STALE = 2 * TICK_PERIOD    # two periods with no tick is a ledger that has stopped moving, not a quiet box

PHANTOM_PROC_AGE = 3600           # a process older than this…
PHANTOM_IDLE = 1800               # …whose transcript has not grown in this long is spending on nothing
# "grew this tick" means bytes arrived AND they are fresh. Without this the FIRST tick — which backfills every day
# of history on disk — would report every session this box has ever run as an unwatched phantom.
GREW_WINDOW = 3600


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- rates

def rate(model, fast=False):
    """The row for a model id. `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` and `claude-opus-5[1m]` are the same families as their
    bare names — Claude Code writes both spellings — so the match is on the longest prefix, suffix stripped."""
    m = re.sub(r"\[[^\]]*\]\s*$", "", (model or "").strip())
    for fam in sorted(RATES, key=len, reverse=True):
        if m.startswith(fam):
            return (FAST.get(fam) if fast else None) or RATES[fam]
    return None


def usd(model, u, fast=False):
    """The estimate for one usage block. Returns None for a model with no rates row, so callers can say so."""
    r = rate(model, fast)
    if r is None:
        return None
    rin, rout, rread = r
    cw = int(u.get("cache_creation_input_tokens") or 0)
    cc = u.get("cache_creation") or {}
    w1 = int(cc.get("ephemeral_1h_input_tokens") or 0)
    w5 = int(cc.get("ephemeral_5m_input_tokens") or 0)
    if w5 + w1 != cw:                      # no split recorded, or a partial one: trust the 1 h count, rest is 5 m
        w1, w5 = min(w1, cw), max(cw - w1, 0)
    return (int(u.get("input_tokens") or 0) * rin
            + int(u.get("output_tokens") or 0) * rout
            + int(u.get("cache_read_input_tokens") or 0) * rread
            + w5 * rin * W_5M + w1 * rin * W_1H) / 1e6


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- where a cwd belongs

def place(cwd):
    """(repo, track) for a working directory. ~/.cc/worktrees/<repo>/<track> is a worker; ~/dev/<repo> is that
    repo's planning session; ~/dev/<handle> carrying .cc/member-workspace is a member's own world; ~/.cc/steward/
    <repo> is a headless steward run, called by nobody's window. The track column holds the track name, or the
    word that says which of the others it is — `session`, `member`, `steward` — so `--by track` shows a repo's
    steward runs as their own line beside its planning session, once such runs exist.

    A review or a gate round runs in a scratch checkout instead, `cc-land.<step>.<repo>.<pr>.<random>`, under the
    run's own root `/tmp/cc-land.run.<pid>.<random>` (since 2026-09-11; before that, straight under /tmp) — the
    name is cc-land head_checkout()'s side of this, and there is nothing else to read, because the directory is
    gone by the time a tick sees the transcript. The step is the track: `<repo>/review` is the repo's money. The
    FIRST part of the path that carries the contract wins, so the run root above it, which does not, is walked
    past. A scratch directory whose name predates that contract, or that a rename breaks, still says LANDING
    rather than nothing — an unattributable quarter of a day's spend reads as a mistake, an unattributable
    landing does not."""
    cwd = (cwd or "").rstrip("/")
    for base, kind in ((WTS, "worktree"), (DEV, "dev"), (STEWARD, "steward")):
        if cwd == base or cwd.startswith(base + "/"):
            parts = cwd[len(base) + 1:].split("/") if cwd != base else []
            if not parts or not parts[0]:
                return "-", "-"
            if kind == "worktree":
                return parts[0], (parts[1] if len(parts) > 1 else "-")
            if kind == "steward":
                return parts[0], "steward"
            if os.path.exists(os.path.join(DEV, parts[0], ".cc", "member-workspace")):
                return parts[0], "member"
            return parts[0], "session"
    scratch = False
    for part in cwd.split("/"):                      # the checkout itself, or a gate running one level down in it
        if part.startswith(SCRATCH):
            m = re.fullmatch(rf"{SCRATCH}\.([a-z]+)\.(.+)\.[^.]*\.[^.]*", part)
            if m and m.group(1) != "run":            # …and never the run root, which names a pid and no repo
                return m.group(2), m.group(1)
            scratch = True
    return ("-", SCRATCH_LANE) if scratch else ("-", "-")


def kind_of(rec, cwd):
    """interactive or headless. `entrypoint` is the record's own answer — 'cli' is a person at a terminal, the
    SDK spellings are a `claude -p`. Older records carry no entrypoint; there, a worktree means a worker."""
    ep = rec.get("entrypoint")
    if ep:
        return "interactive" if ep == "cli" else "headless"
    return "headless" if (cwd or "").startswith(WTS + "/") else "interactive"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- state files

def iso(dt):
    return dt.astimezone(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


def now():
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc)


def parse_ts(s):
    try:
        return datetime.fromisoformat((s or "").replace("Z", "+00:00")).astimezone(timezone.utc)
    except ValueError:
        return None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the box's own day

# Everything the OWNER reads rolls at the day the box is in, so moving the box is one system setting
# (`timedatectl set-timezone`) and no edit to any config this repo owns. The LEDGER does not move: a row keeps
# its UTC `ts` and lands in the UTC-named day file, because the ledger is what reconciliation compares against
# the logs and the boards, and mixing the two is where that has broken before. Only the views re-bucket.

DAY_BOUNDARY = "local"   # what `--json` reports it did. The shape before this change said "utc".


def tz_name():
    """The IANA name of the zone the box is in, off the system alone: $TZ, else what /etc/localtime points at,
    else /etc/timezone. None of those is a config this repo owns, which is the point."""
    t = (os.environ.get("TZ") or "").strip().lstrip(":")
    if not t:
        real = os.path.realpath("/etc/localtime")
        i = real.find("/zoneinfo/")
        if i >= 0:
            t = real[i + len("/zoneinfo/"):]
    if not t:
        try:
            with open("/etc/timezone") as f:
                t = f.read().strip()
        except OSError:
            t = ""
    return t or "UTC"


_TZ = None


def tz():
    """(IANA name, tzinfo), resolved once per process — a view converts thousands of rows and must not stat
    /etc/localtime for each. A name the tz database does not know falls back to UTC UNDER ITS OWN NAME, so a
    payload never claims a zone it did not actually bucket by."""
    global _TZ
    if _TZ is None:
        name = tz_name()
        try:
            _TZ = (name, ZoneInfo(name))
        except (KeyError, ValueError, OSError):
            _TZ = ("UTC", timezone.utc)
    return _TZ


def local_day(ts):
    """The box-local date a UTC stamp fell on. It converts the INSTANT and reads the date off the result; no
    arithmetic on a 24-hour span happens anywhere here, which is the only reason a 23- or 25-hour DST day
    buckets correctly."""
    d = ts if isinstance(ts, datetime) else parse_ts(ts)
    return d.astimezone(tz()[1]).strftime("%Y-%m-%d") if d else None


def local_days(n, end=None):
    """The last n box-local dates, newest first. DATE arithmetic, not datetime: subtracting a day from an aware
    datetime is 24 absolute hours, so on a spring-forward morning that lands back on the date it started on."""
    d = (end or now()).astimezone(tz()[1]).date()
    return [(d - timedelta(days=i)).strftime("%Y-%m-%d") for i in range(n)]


def ledger_days(window):
    """The UTC day files that can hold a local window's rows, newest first. Every zone is less than a whole day
    from UTC, so it is the window plus one day at each end; the extra file costs one read and never a wrong
    bucket, because read_local_rows() filters on the local date regardless."""
    ds = [datetime.strptime(d, "%Y-%m-%d").date() for d in window]
    d, lo, out = max(ds) + timedelta(days=1), min(ds) - timedelta(days=1), []
    while d >= lo:
        out.append(d.strftime("%Y-%m-%d"))
        d -= timedelta(days=1)
    return out


def read_local_rows(window):
    """Every ledger row falling on one of these BOX-LOCAL dates, read out of the UTC files that can hold them.
    Each row gains a `day`: the local date it was bucketed under, so no caller re-derives it."""
    keep = set(window)
    for r in read_rows(ledger_days(window)):
        # A hand-edited, unparseable stamp buckets by its own literal prefix, exactly as bucketing by the
        # file name used to — dropping it silently would lose money out of a view.
        d = local_day(r["ts"]) or r["ts"][:10]
        if d in keep:
            r["day"] = d
            yield r


def totals(rows):
    """One bucket's figures. `auto` is kind == "headless" — a `claude -p` nobody is sitting in front of."""
    t = {"usd": 0.0, "auto_usd": 0.0, "in": 0, "out": 0, "cache_read": 0, "cache_write": 0, "rows": 0}
    for r in rows:
        t["rows"] += 1
        t["usd"] += r["est_usd"]
        if r["kind"] == "headless":
            t["auto_usd"] += r["est_usd"]
        for k in ("in", "out", "cache_read", "cache_write"):
            t[k] += r[k]
    t["usd"], t["auto_usd"] = round(t["usd"], 6), round(t["auto_usd"], 6)
    return t


def load_offsets():
    try:
        with open(OFF) as f:
            d = json.load(f)
        return d if isinstance(d, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}


def save_json(path, obj):
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(obj, f)
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def cell(v):
    """A path or a model name with a tab or a newline in it would silently shift every column after it."""
    return re.sub(r"[\t\r\n]+", " ", str(v))


def day_path(day):
    return os.path.join(ST, f"{day}.tsv")


def append_rows(rows):
    """One open-and-write per day file, and each write is a single append: two ticks that overlap interleave
    whole rows rather than half a line."""
    by_day = {}
    for r in rows:
        by_day.setdefault(r[0][:10], []).append("\t".join(cell(c) for c in r))
    os.makedirs(ST, exist_ok=True)
    for day, lines in by_day.items():
        p = day_path(day)
        head = "" if os.path.exists(p) else "#" + "\t".join(COLS) + "\n"
        with open(p, "a") as f:
            f.write(head + "\n".join(lines) + "\n")


def read_rows(days):
    for day in days:
        try:
            fh = open(day_path(day))
        except OSError:
            continue
        with fh:
            for line in fh:
                if line.startswith("#") or not line.strip():
                    continue
                p = line.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
                if len(p) != len(COLS):
                    continue
                r = dict(zip(COLS, p))
                try:                       # a half-written or hand-edited row is skipped, never a crash mid-view
                    for k in ("in", "out", "cache_read", "cache_write"):
                        r[k] = int(r[k] or 0)
                    r["est_usd"] = float(r["est_usd"] or 0)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                yield r


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the tick

def is_session_transcript(path):
    """True for a session's own <project>/<session>.jsonl, false for a subagent's, which sits one level
    deeper under <session>/subagents/."""
    return os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(path)) == PROJ


def scan_file(path, prev, seen_rids=None, sub_rids=None):
    """Read one transcript from where the last tick stopped. Returns (rows, newstate, rids) where rows are
    ((session, model, day) -> totals), newstate is what to remember about this file, and rids is every
    (session, requestId) an assistant record in the NEW bytes just read carried — whether or not it was
    counted — so a caller walking a subagent's own file can fold them into the persisted registry of requestIds
    a subagent transcript actually holds, incrementally, without ever re-reading a byte twice.

    seen_rids, when given, is the tick's set of (session, requestId): a request written into more than one file
    of the same session is counted once, whichever file the walk reaches first. sub_rids, given only for a
    session's own <session>.jsonl, is that persisted registry — {session: {requestId, ...}} — and an isSidechain
    record is dropped only when its OWN requestId is in its OWN session's set, because that is the one case
    where a subagent's file really holds the same record. Every other isSidechain record — no subagents/
    directory at all, or one whose files don't carry this requestId — is counted: dropping it on layout alone,
    as before, just lost the tokens."""
    stt = os.stat(path)
    start = int(prev.get("off") or 0)
    if stt.st_ino != prev.get("ino") or stt.st_size < start:
        start, prev = 0, {}                    # truncated, replaced, or never seen: the offset means nothing
    state = {"off": start, "ino": stt.st_ino, "rid": prev.get("rid", "")}
    if stt.st_size <= start:
        return {}, state, set()
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        f.seek(start)
        blob = f.read()
    cut = blob.rfind(b"\n")
    if cut < 0:                                # a record still being written: leave the whole thing for next tick
        return {}, state, set()
    state["off"] = start + cut + 1
    seen = {state["rid"]} if state["rid"] else set()
    agg, rids = {}, set()
    for line in blob[:cut + 1].decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines():
        if '"assistant"' not in line:
            continue
        try:
            rec = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if rec.get("type") != "assistant":
            continue
        msg = rec.get("message") or {}
        u = msg.get("usage") or {}
        rid = rec.get("requestId") or msg.get("id") or ""
        sid = rec.get("sessionId") or os.path.basename(path)[:-6]
        if rid:
            state["rid"] = rid                 # remembered even when skipped: it is still the last one read
            rids.add((sid, rid))
        if rec.get("isSidechain") and sub_rids is not None and rid in (sub_rids.get(sid) or ()):
            continue                           # a subagent file really holds this record; count it there instead
        if rid:
            if rid in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(rid)
            if seen_rids is not None:
                if (sid, rid) in seen_rids:
                    continue                   # already counted from another file of this same session
                seen_rids.add((sid, rid))
        model = msg.get("model") or "-"
        tok = [int(u.get(k) or 0) for k in
               ("input_tokens", "output_tokens", "cache_read_input_tokens", "cache_creation_input_tokens")]
        if not any(tok):
            continue
        ts = parse_ts(rec.get("timestamp")) or datetime.fromtimestamp(stt.st_mtime, timezone.utc)
        cwd = rec.get("cwd") or "-"
        key = (sid, model, iso(ts)[:10])
        a = agg.setdefault(key, {"ts": ts, "cwd": cwd, "kind": kind_of(rec, cwd), "tok": [0, 0, 0, 0], "usd": 0.0})
        a["ts"] = max(a["ts"], ts)
        a["cwd"], a["kind"] = cwd, kind_of(rec, cwd)
        for i in range(4):
            a["tok"][i] += tok[i]
        a["usd"] += usd(model, u, (u.get("speed") == "fast")) or 0.0
    return agg, state, rids


def proc_snapshot():
    """Every live `claude`, the way cc-reconcile walks /proc: comm, cwd through /proc/<pid>/cwd, and the argv that
    says headless or interactive. No `ps`, so nothing depends on its output format."""
    try:
        with open("/proc/uptime") as f:
            up = float(f.read().split()[0])
    except OSError:
        return []
    tck = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK")
    out = []
    for pid in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not pid.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            if open(f"/proc/{pid}/comm").read().strip() != "claude":
                continue
            argv = open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb").read().decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
            cwd = os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/cwd")
            stat = open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat").read()
            ppid = next(ln.split()[1] for ln in open(f"/proc/{pid}/status") if ln.startswith("PPid:"))
        except (OSError, StopIteration):
            continue                           # gone between listdir and here, or not ours to read
        try:
            age = int(up - int(stat[stat.rfind(")") + 2:].split()[19]) / tck)
        except (ValueError, IndexError):
            age = 0
        headless = any(a in ("-p", "--print") or a.startswith("--max-budget-usd") for a in argv)
        model = sid = "-"
        for i, a in enumerate(argv):
            if a == "--model" and i + 1 < len(argv):
                model = argv[i + 1]
            if a in ("--session-id", "--resume") and i + 1 < len(argv):
                sid = argv[i + 1]
        repo, track = place(cwd)
        out.append({"pid": int(pid), "ppid": int(ppid), "etime_s": max(age, 0), "cwd": cwd, "repo": repo,
                    "track": track, "kind": "headless" if headless else "interactive", "model": model,
                    "session": sid})
    return sorted(out, key=lambda p: -p["etime_s"])


def tick():
    os.makedirs(ST, exist_ok=True)
    lock = open(os.path.join(ST, ".tick.lock"), "w")
    try:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except OSError:
        print("cc-spend: another tick holds the lock — nothing to do")
        return 0
    stamp = now()
    off = load_offsets()
    files = off.get("files") or {}
    sub_rids = {sid: set(rids) for sid, rids in (off.get("sub_rids") or {}).items()}   # {session: {requestId}}
    rows, grew, seen_files = [], {}, set()
    seen_rids = set()                          # (session, requestId) across every file this tick reads
    # Recursive: a SUBAGENT writes its own transcript one level deeper, <project>/<session>/subagents/*.jsonl.
    # A one-level glob missed those, and most of a busy hour's spend now runs in subagents.
    # The two layouts do not overlap on this box, measured 2026-09-04 on Claude Code 2.1.261: of 1219 parent
    # transcripts under ~/.claude/projects not one holds an isSidechain record, and for the live session
    # 94a32efb (438 assistant records with usage, all isSidechain false) its 20 subagent files carry 477
    # distinct requestIds, 0 of them also in the parent. The cross-file requestId set below costs nothing today
    # and keeps the count right if a version writes the same record in both places — but a parent's own
    # isSidechain record is real spend the day it has no subagent file to answer for it, so it is dropped only
    # when sub_rids proves a copy of THAT requestId actually exists, never on layout alone. sub_rids is built
    # from every subagent file's OWN scan (never a separate whole-file read) and kept in offsets.json, keyed by
    # session so a prune is one dict delete, so a subagent transcript read in an earlier tick still answers for
    # a sidechain copy the parent writes later — and subagent files are walked before session transcripts below
    # so a same-tick pair is caught too. Pruned below once a session's subagents/ files are gone from the walk:
    # kept forever this would be hundreds of thousands of pairs, most of them sessions long done.
    paths = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(PROJ, "**", "*.jsonl"), recursive=True),
                   key=lambda p: (is_session_transcript(p), p))
    live_sub_sessions = set()                  # sessions this walk still finds a subagents/ file under
    for path in paths:
        seen_files.add(path)
        is_parent = is_session_transcript(path)
        try:
            agg, state, rids = scan_file(path, files.get(path) or {}, seen_rids,
                                         sub_rids=sub_rids if is_parent else None)
        except OSError:
            continue                           # deleted or unreadable mid-walk: the next tick picks it up
        files[path] = state
        if not is_parent:
            live_sub_sessions.add(os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(path))))
            for s, r in rids:                  # this subagent file's newly-read requestIds, folded in for the
                sub_rids.setdefault(s, set()).add(r)  # rest of this tick and every one after
        for (sid, model, _day), a in agg.items():
            i, o, cr, cw = a["tok"]
            repo, track = place(a["cwd"])
            rows.append([iso(a["ts"]), sid, a["cwd"], repo, track, a["kind"], model, i, o, cr, cw,
                         f"{a['usd']:.6f}"])
            if (stamp - a["ts"]).total_seconds() <= GREW_WINDOW:
                g = grew.setdefault(sid, {"cwd": a["cwd"], "repo": repo, "track": track, "kind": a["kind"],
                                          "usd": 0.0, "tok": 0, "ts": iso(a["ts"])})
                g["usd"] += a["usd"]
                g["tok"] += i + o + cr + cw
                g["ts"] = max(g["ts"], iso(a["ts"]))
    for gone in set(files) - seen_files:       # a transcript that vanished takes its offset with it
        del files[gone]
    for done in set(sub_rids) - live_sub_sessions:   # its subagents/ files are gone from this walk too — a
        del sub_rids[done]                           # prune of the registry, not of anything already counted
    rows.sort()
    # Rows first, offsets second: a crash between them re-reads and duplicates a tick's rows, where the other
    # order would lose them outright. Over-counting once is visible and fixable; a hole in the ledger is not.
    append_rows(rows)
    procs = proc_snapshot()
    save_tsv(PROCS, PCOLS, [[iso(stamp)] + [p[c] for c in PCOLS[1:]] for p in procs])
    save_json(OFF, {"tick": iso(stamp), "files": files, "grew": grew,
                    "sub_rids": {sid: sorted(rids) for sid, rids in sub_rids.items()}})
    # …and the phantom list, last: it reads procs.tsv and the rows this tick just appended, so it must run after
    # both are on disk. A reader of the file gets the same answer `cc-spend phantoms` would give.
    ph = phantom_now(grew, stamp)
    save_tsv(PHANTOMS, PHCOLS, [[iso(stamp)] + [r[c] for c in PHCOLS[1:]] for r in ph])
    print(f"cc-spend tick {iso(stamp)}: {len(rows)} row(s), {len(grew)} session(s) grew, "
          f"{len(procs)} live claude, {len(ph)} phantom(s), ${sum(float(r[-1]) for r in rows):.4f} est")
    return 0


def save_tsv(path, cols, rows):
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        f.write("#" + "\t".join(cols) + "\n")
        for r in rows:
            f.write("\t".join(cell(c) for c in r) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def read_tsv(path, cols):
    try:
        fh = open(path)
    except OSError:
        return []
    out = []
    with fh:
        for line in fh:
            if line.startswith("#") or not line.strip():
                continue
            p = line.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
            if len(p) == len(cols):
                out.append(dict(zip(cols, p)))
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- phantoms

def tmux_paths():
    import subprocess
    try:
        o = subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F", "#{pane_current_path}"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return set()
    return {ln.strip().rstrip("/") for ln in o.stdout.splitlines() if ln.strip()}


def loop_tracks():
    """`repo/track` for every live `cc-loop <repo> <track>` — the one thing still alive while a loop waits out a
    usage limit, so a board row without it is not being carried by anything."""
    live = set()
    for pid in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not pid.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            argv = open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb").read().decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
        except OSError:
            continue
        for i, a in enumerate(argv):
            if a and os.path.basename(a) == "cc-loop" and len(argv) > i + 2 and argv[i + 1] and argv[i + 2]:
                live.add(f"{argv[i + 1]}/{argv[i + 2]}")
                break
    return live


def board_read(path):
    """A board file, read through cc-board's opener (imported, the way cc-time is): a member's board is a link into a
    directory the MEMBER writes, and a plain open here followed whatever it planted — a pipe hung this tool for good
    (review of #224). None = no board there; cc-board's Refused for anything planted, which a caller treats as unreadable."""
    if not hasattr(board_read, "mod"):
        import importlib.machinery, importlib.util
        s = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("ccboard", importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
            "ccboard", os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-board")))   # beside THIS copy, not a stubbed BIN
        board_read.mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(s); s.loader.exec_module(board_read.mod)
    return board_read.mod.read(path)


def board_states():
    out = {}
    for f in glob.glob(os.path.join(BOARDS, "*.json")):
        try:
            d = board_read(f)
        except Exception:
            continue
        if d is None:   # nothing at the end of it: a dangling link, or a board removed between the glob and this read.
            continue    # `.get` on that killed every 5-minute run of this tool with an AttributeError (review of #224)
        repo = d.get("repo") or os.path.basename(f)[:-5]
        for track, t in (d.get("tracks") or {}).items():
            out[f"{repo}/{track}"] = t.get("status", "")
    return out


def watched(cwd, repo, track, panes, loops, boards):
    """Is anyone watching this spend? A tmux window on the directory counts, and so does a board row in a live
    state WITH a loop alive behind it — a row saying `running` with nothing running is exactly the case this
    command exists to catch.

    A pane watches its own directory and anything it has cd'd DEEPER into, never a parent: this box's `box`
    window sits permanently at ~/dev (tmux-main.service runs cc-rc from there), so counting an ancestor pane
    would mark every ~/dev/<repo> and ~/dev/<member>/... session watched and rule 1 would never fire again."""
    c = (cwd or "").rstrip("/")
    for p in panes:
        if c == p or p.startswith(c + "/"):
            return True
    key = f"{repo}/{track}"
    return boards.get(key) in ("running", "review") and key in loops


def phantom_rows(grew, procs, panes, loops, boards, last_growth, at):
    """Pure, so the selfcheck can prove both rules without a tmux server, a board or a live process."""
    out = []
    for sid, g in sorted(grew.items(), key=lambda kv: -float(kv[1].get("usd") or 0)):
        if watched(g.get("cwd"), g.get("repo"), g.get("track"), panes, loops, boards):
            continue
        out.append({"why": "unwatched", "what": sid[:8], "cwd": g.get("cwd", "-"),
                    "repo": g.get("repo", "-"), "track": g.get("track", "-"), "kind": g.get("kind", "-"),
                    "detail": f"grew this tick, ${float(g.get('usd') or 0):.4f}, nobody watching"})
    for p in procs:
        age = int(p.get("etime_s") or 0)
        if age < PHANTOM_PROC_AGE:
            continue
        last = last_growth.get((p.get("cwd") or "").rstrip("/"))
        idle = (at - last).total_seconds() if last else None
        if idle is not None and idle < PHANTOM_IDLE:
            continue
        say = "no transcript row at all" if idle is None else f"transcript idle {idle / 3600:.1f} h"
        # An idle process in a window is a session someone left sitting; one with nothing on it is the leak. The
        # rule catches both — this says which, so the list stays worth reading.
        who = "in a window" if watched(p.get("cwd"), p.get("repo"), p.get("track"), panes, loops, boards) \
            else "nobody watching"
        out.append({"why": "idle", "what": f"pid {p['pid']}", "cwd": p.get("cwd", "-"),
                    "repo": p.get("repo", "-"), "track": p.get("track", "-"), "kind": p.get("kind", "-"),
                    "detail": f"alive {age / 3600:.1f} h, {say}, {who}"})
    return out


def phantom_now(grew, at):
    """The phantom list as it stands right now. The tick calls it with the `grew` it just built and saves the
    answer; `cc-spend phantoms` calls it with the `grew` on disk and prints it. One rule, one implementation."""
    growth = {}
    for r in read_rows(days(2, at)):
        t = parse_ts(r["ts"])
        c = r["cwd"].rstrip("/")
        if t and (c not in growth or t > growth[c]):
            growth[c] = t
    return phantom_rows(grew, read_tsv(PROCS, PCOLS), tmux_paths(), loop_tracks(), board_states(), growth, at)


def phantoms():
    off = load_offsets()
    if not off.get("tick"):
        print("cc-spend: no tick has run yet — `cc-spend tick` or switch cc-spend.timer on")
        return 1
    at = now()
    rows = phantom_now(off.get("grew") or {}, at)
    print(f"phantoms — last tick {off['tick']}")
    if not rows:
        print("  none: every session that grew has a window or a live board row, and no process is idling")
        return 0
    table([[r["why"], r["what"], f"{r['repo']}/{r['track']}", r["kind"], r["detail"], r["cwd"]] for r in rows],
          ["why", "what", "repo/track", "kind", "detail", "cwd"])
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- views

def days(n, end=None):
    end = end or now()
    return [(end - timedelta(days=i)).strftime("%Y-%m-%d") for i in range(n)]


def hn(n):
    n = int(n)
    if abs(n) >= 1_000_000:
        return f"{n / 1_000_000:.1f}M"
    if abs(n) >= 1_000:
        return f"{n / 1_000:.1f}k"
    return str(n)


def table(rows, head):
    """Returns the column widths, so a caller can line a total up under them."""
    w = [len(h) for h in head]
    for r in rows:
        for i, c in enumerate(r):
            w[i] = max(w[i], len(str(c)))
    print("  " + "  ".join(h.ljust(w[i]) for i, h in enumerate(head)))
    for r in rows:
        print("  " + "  ".join(str(c).ljust(w[i]) for i, c in enumerate(r)))
    return w


def crosscheck(window):
    """The workers' own results are the second source: each headless run writes total_cost_usd and the session it
    ran in. The comparison is against THOSE sessions only — most headless spend on this box (gate runs, audits,
    subagents) never writes a runs/ file, so holding the whole headless column up against them would read as a
    2x error when it is really the ledger seeing what the runs/ files never did.

    Both sides are WHOLE SESSIONS, which is the only like-for-like there is: total_cost_usd covers a run end to
    end and cannot be split by day, while the ledger dates each row by the record's own timestamp. Comparing a
    run that finished today against today's ledger rows alone charged the run's whole life to its last day —
    that is the $116-vs-$4.78 the first real tick reported. So the window only PICKS the sessions (a run file
    touched in it); the sum then takes every run those sessions ever wrote, and view() sums the ledger for them
    over every day on disk."""
    per = {}                               # session -> [usd end to end, run files, touched the window]
    for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(STATE, "*", "*", "runs", "*.json"))):
        try:
            d = json.load(open(f))
            sid = d.get("session_id")
            if not sid:
                continue
            day = datetime.fromtimestamp(os.path.getmtime(f), timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
        except (OSError, ValueError, TypeError):
            continue                       # a run still in flight writes an empty file; skip it, never crash
        per.setdefault(sid, [0.0, 0, False])
        per[sid][0] += float(d.get("total_cost_usd") or 0)
        per[sid][1] += 1
        per[sid][2] = per[sid][2] or day in window
    sids = {s for s, v in per.items() if v[2]}
    tot = sum(per[s][0] for s in sids)
    n = sum(per[s][1] for s in sids)
    return tot, n, sids


def all_days():
    """Every day the ledger has on disk, newest first — the span a whole-session sum has to cover."""
    return sorted((os.path.basename(p)[:-4] for p in glob.glob(os.path.join(ST, "*.tsv"))
                   if re.fullmatch(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}", os.path.basename(p)[:-4])), reverse=True)


def view(by, window):
    """`window` is a list of BOX-LOCAL dates, newest first — what the owner means by "today" and "this week"."""
    rows = list(read_local_rows(window))
    span = window[0] if len(window) == 1 else f"{window[-1]}..{window[0]}"
    if not rows:
        print(f"cc-spend: nothing recorded for {span}"
              + ("" if os.path.exists(OFF) else " — no tick has run yet"))
        return 0
    groups, unpriced = {}, 0
    for r in rows:
        if by == "session":
            key = r["session"][:8]
        elif by == "repo":
            key = r["repo"]
        elif by == "track":
            key = f"{r['repo']}/{r['track']}"
        elif by == "model":
            key = r["model"]
        else:
            key = r["kind"]
        g = groups.setdefault(key, {"usd": 0.0, "in": 0, "out": 0, "cache_read": 0, "cache_write": 0,
                                    "where": set(), "models": set()})
        g["usd"] += r["est_usd"]
        for k in ("in", "out", "cache_read", "cache_write"):
            g[k] += r[k]
        g["where"].add(f"{r['repo']}/{r['track']} {r['kind']}")
        g["models"].add(r["model"])
        if rate(r["model"]) is None:
            unpriced += 1
    order = sorted(groups.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]["usd"])
    head = [by, "est $", "in", "out", "cache rd", "cache wr"] + (["where", "model"] if by == "session" else [])
    out = []
    for k, g in order:
        row = [k, f"{g['usd']:.4f}", hn(g["in"]), hn(g["out"]), hn(g["cache_read"]), hn(g["cache_write"])]
        if by == "session":
            row += [", ".join(sorted(g["where"]))[:44], ", ".join(sorted(g["models"]))[:36]]
        out.append(row)
    print(f"cc-spend {span} ({tz()[0]}) — by {by}, {len(rows)} row(s). Days roll at the box's own midnight. "
          f"USD is an ESTIMATE; the token columns are the truth.")
    w = table(out, head)
    tot = sum(g["usd"] for g in groups.values())
    print(f"  {'total'.ljust(w[0])}  {f'{tot:.4f}'.ljust(w[1])}  <- estimate")
    # crosscheck only PICKS sessions, by a run file's UTC mtime day — see its docstring. The covering UTC span
    # is a superset of the local window, which is what a selector wants; the sum then takes every day on disk.
    cc, n, sids = crosscheck(ledger_days(window))
    if n:
        # Whole run against whole session: see crosscheck(). Summing only the rows in `rows` would charge a
        # multi-day run entirely to the day it ended and report a gap that is not there.
        mine = sum(r["est_usd"] for r in read_rows(all_days()) if r["session"] in sids)
        print(f"  cross-check: {n} worker run(s) over {len(sids)} session(s) report ${cc:.4f} end to end; this "
              f"ledger estimates ${mine:.4f} for those sessions across all days (a run cannot be split by day)")
    if unpriced:
        print(f"  ({unpriced} row(s) from models with no rates row, counted in tokens and estimated at $0)")
    return 0


def cmd_cache(n):
    """`cc-spend cache [--days N]` — cache-read tokens per (session, day), biggest first, off the same TSVs
    every other view reads. This is the number a steward is judged by: not a fresh collector, the ledger's own
    cache_read column, which `--by session` already carries but only a day at a time."""
    window = local_days(n)
    rows = list(read_local_rows(window))
    span = window[0] if len(window) == 1 else f"{window[-1]}..{window[0]}"
    if not rows:
        print(f"cc-spend: nothing recorded for {span}"
              + ("" if os.path.exists(OFF) else " — no tick has run yet"))
        return 0
    groups = {}
    for r in rows:
        key = (r["session"][:8], r["day"])
        g = groups.setdefault(key, {"usd": 0.0, "in": 0, "out": 0, "cache_read": 0, "cache_write": 0})
        g["usd"] += r["est_usd"]
        for k in ("in", "out", "cache_read", "cache_write"):
            g[k] += r[k]
    order = sorted(groups.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]["cache_read"])
    head = ["session", "day", "cache rd", "cache wr", "in", "out", "est $", "rd/out"]
    out = []
    for (sid, day), g in order:
        ratio = f"{g['cache_read'] / g['out']:.1f}" if g["out"] else "-"
        out.append([sid, day, hn(g["cache_read"]), hn(g["cache_write"]), hn(g["in"]), hn(g["out"]),
                    f"{g['usd']:.4f}", ratio])
    print(f"cc-spend cache {span} ({tz()[0]}) — {len(rows)} row(s), by session/local day, cache read biggest "
          f"first. USD is an ESTIMATE.")
    w = table(out, head)
    tcr = sum(g["cache_read"] for g in groups.values())
    tcw = sum(g["cache_write"] for g in groups.values())
    tin = sum(g["in"] for g in groups.values())
    tout = sum(g["out"] for g in groups.values())
    tusd = sum(g["usd"] for g in groups.values())
    tratio = f"{tcr / tout:.1f}" if tout else "-"
    print("  " + "  ".join([
        "total".ljust(w[0]), "".ljust(w[1]), hn(tcr).ljust(w[2]), hn(tcw).ljust(w[3]),
        hn(tin).ljust(w[4]), hn(tout).ljust(w[5]), f"{tusd:.4f}".ljust(w[6]), tratio.ljust(w[7])]))
    return 0


def day_totals(day):
    """{repo: {"auto": usd, "all": usd}} for ONE BOX-LOCAL day, off the UTC ledger files that can hold it.

    AUTO is kind == "headless" — a `claude -p` nobody is sitting in front of: worker loops, landing rounds,
    audits. Interactive rows are a person at a terminal and are counted in `all` only, because an owner asking
    for something is not what a ceiling on autonomous work is about."""
    tot = {}
    for r in read_local_rows([day]):
        t = tot.setdefault(r["repo"], {"auto": 0.0, "all": 0.0})
        t["all"] += r["est_usd"]
        if r["kind"] == "headless":
            t["auto"] += r["est_usd"]
    return tot


def ledger_age(at=None):
    """(last tick, seconds since it), or (None, None) when no tick has ever run. Nothing in the ledger is more
    current than this, so anything reading a figure off it has to be told."""
    t = parse_ts((load_offsets() or {}).get("tick"))
    return (t, ((at or now()) - t).total_seconds()) if t else (None, None)


def cmd_today(repo=None):
    day = local_days(1)[0]                               # the box's own day, not UTC's
    tot = day_totals(day)
    at, age = ledger_age()
    stale = age is None or age > LEDGER_STALE
    if at is None:
        note = (f"no tick has run yet, so {day} reads as $0 because nothing has been measured — not because "
                f"nothing was spent (`cc-spend tick`, or switch cc-spend.timer on)")
    elif stale:
        note = (f"the ledger has not ticked since {iso(at)} ({int(age // 60)} min ago) — this is what it knew "
                f"then, not what has been spent since")
    else:
        note = f"last tick {iso(at)}"
    if repo:
        # ONE number on stdout, so a caller reads it whole: `spent=$(cc-spend today "$repo")`. A repo with no
        # rows today has spent nothing, which is 0 and not an error. The caveat goes to stderr and the exit
        # status, so a caller that ignores both still gets a figure and a person still sees why it may be low.
        print(f"{tot.get(repo, {}).get('auto', 0.0):.4f}")
        if stale:
            print(f"cc-spend: {note}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1 if stale else 0
    print(f"cc-spend {day} ({tz()[0]}) — what AUTONOMOUS work has booked today; `all` adds the interactive "
          f"sessions beside it. USD is an ESTIMATE. {note[0].upper() + note[1:]}.")
    rows = sorted(tot.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]["auto"])
    if not rows:
        print("  nothing recorded")
        return 1 if stale else 0
    w = table([[k, f"{v['auto']:.4f}", f"{v['all']:.4f}"] for k, v in rows], ["repo", "auto $", "all $"])
    a, b = sum(v["auto"] for _, v in rows), sum(v["all"] for _, v in rows)
    print(f"  {'total'.ljust(w[0])}  {f'{a:.4f}'.ljust(w[1])}  {b:.4f}")
    return 1 if stale else 0


def cmd_json():
    """`cc-spend --json` — the same figures for a machine, agreed with the dashboard on 2026-09-07 and not this
    file's to change alone: `tz` is the IANA name, `day_boundary` says WHICH boundary produced the numbers so a
    consumer labels what it got instead of inferring it from a version, and `today`, `week` and `by_day` are
    bucketed by the box's local day with `by_day` keyed on local dates.

    It exits 0 even on a frozen ledger, unlike `today`: staleness is `stale` and `last_tick` in the payload, and
    a reader that raises on a non-zero status would throw away a complete answer over a field it can read."""
    zone = tz()[0]
    window = local_days(7)                               # `week`, and the keys of `by_day`
    per = {d: [] for d in window}
    for r in read_local_rows(window):
        per[r["day"]].append(r)
    at, age = ledger_age()
    print(json.dumps({
        "tz": zone,
        "day_boundary": DAY_BOUNDARY,
        "generated": iso(now()),
        "last_tick": iso(at) if at else None,
        "stale": age is None or age > LEDGER_STALE,
        "today": totals(per[window[0]]),
        "week": totals([r for d in window for r in per[d]]),
        "by_day": {d: totals(per[d]) for d in window},
    }, indent=2))
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- selfcheck

def selfcheck():
    """STATIC: fixtures in a HOME of its own — a synthetic transcript, a runs/ json, an offsets file. No network,
    no model, and it never reads or writes this box's ledger."""
    import shutil
    import subprocess
    import tempfile
    n = [0]

    def ok(what, cond):
        print(("  ok   " if cond else "  FAIL ") + what)
        if not cond:
            n[0] += 1

    print("cc-spend selfcheck")
    # ---- the rates table, against the numbers Claude Code itself reports
    ok("a model id with a date or a [1m] suffix finds its family",
       rate("claude-haiku-4-5-20251001") == RATES["claude-haiku-4-5"]
       and rate("claude-opus-5[1m]") == RATES["claude-opus-5"] and rate("no-such-model") is None)
    ok("fast mode is priced as fast mode, not as the standard model",
       rate("claude-opus-5", True) == FAST["claude-opus-5"] and rate("claude-sonnet-5", True) == RATES["claude-sonnet-5"])
    u1h = {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0,
           "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1_000_000,
           "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1_000_000, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}}
    u5m = dict(u1h, cache_creation={"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1_000_000})
    ok("a cache write is priced by its TTL — 2x input at 1 h, 1.25x at 5 min",
       abs(usd("claude-opus-5", u1h) - 10.0) < 1e-9 and abs(usd("claude-opus-5", u5m) - 6.25) < 1e-9)
    ok("…and a write with no TTL split recorded is priced as the cheaper 5-minute one",
       abs(usd("claude-opus-5", {"cache_creation_input_tokens": 1_000_000}) - 6.25) < 1e-9)
    ok("Claude Fable 5.1 reads cache at 0.025x input, not the usual 0.1x",
       abs(usd("claude-fable-5-1", {"cache_read_input_tokens": 1_000_000}) - 0.25) < 1e-9
       and abs(usd("claude-fable-5", {"cache_read_input_tokens": 1_000_000}) - 1.0) < 1e-9)
    ok("an unknown model estimates nothing rather than guessing",
       usd("gpt-nonesuch", {"output_tokens": 10}) is None)

    # ---- the lane a cwd resolves to. Pure: no HOME fixture, nothing on disk is read.
    ok("a review or gate round in a landing's scratch checkout is charged to the repo it ran for, not to a dash",
       place("/tmp/cc-land.review.myrepo.7.ab12cd34") == ("myrepo", "review")
       and place("/tmp/cc-land.gates.myrepo.7.ab12cd34/head") == ("myrepo", "gates")
       and place("/tmp/cc-land.review.my.dotted.repo.7.ab12cd34") == ("my.dotted.repo", "review"))
    ok("…a scratch checkout whose name carries no repo still says it was a landing, rather than nothing",
       place("/tmp/cc-land-ab12cd34") == ("-", "landing")
       and place("/tmp/cc-land.review.myrepo") == ("-", "landing"))
    ok("…and one under the run's own root (cc-land.run.<pid>.<random>, since 2026-09-11) is read off the checkout's "
       "name and never off the root's, which names a pid and no repo — a root alone is still a landing",
       place("/tmp/cc-land.run.4242.x1y2z3/cc-land.gates.myrepo.7.ab12cd34/head") == ("myrepo", "gates")
       and place("/tmp/cc-land.run.4242.x1y2z3/cc-land.review.my.dotted.repo.7.ab12cd34") == ("my.dotted.repo", "review")
       and place("/tmp/cc-land.run.4242.x1y2z3") == ("-", "landing"))
    ok("…and a directory that is neither a worktree nor a scratch checkout is still nobody's lane",
       place(f"{WTS}/r/t1") == ("r", "t1") and place("/tmp/some-probe") == ("-", "-") and place("") == ("-", "-"))
    ok("a steward run's cwd is its own track word, beside session and member",
       place(os.path.join(STEWARD, "myrepo")) == ("myrepo", "steward")
       and place(os.path.join(STEWARD, "myrepo", "sub")) == ("myrepo", "steward"))
    ok("…while a plain dev checkout with no steward base and no member marker still reads as a session",
       place(os.path.join(DEV, "no-such-repo-for-selfcheck-xyz")) == ("no-such-repo-for-selfcheck-xyz", "session"))

    th = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ccspend-sc-")
    try:
        me = os.path.realpath(__file__)

        def run(*a):
            return subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, *a], env={**os.environ, "HOME": th},
                                  capture_output=True, text=True)

        proj = os.path.join(th, ".claude", "projects", "-a-worktree-r-t1")   # the name is Claude Code's, not read here
        os.makedirs(proj)
        jl = os.path.join(proj, "s1.jsonl")
        cwd = os.path.join(th, ".cc", "worktrees", "r", "t1")

        def rec(rid, out_tok, ts="2026-09-01T10:00:00.000Z", model="claude-opus-5", ep="sdk-cli"):
            return json.dumps({"type": "assistant", "requestId": rid, "timestamp": ts, "cwd": cwd,
                               "sessionId": "s1", "entrypoint": ep,
                               "message": {"model": model, "id": "m_" + rid,
                                           "usage": {"input_tokens": 100, "output_tokens": out_tok,
                                                     "cache_read_input_tokens": 1000,
                                                     "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}}}) + "\n"

        # THE trap: one API response, three content blocks, three records, each carrying the full usage.
        open(jl, "w").write(rec("req_a", 10) + rec("req_a", 10) + rec("req_a", 10) + rec("req_b", 20))
        r = run("tick")
        rows = [ln.split("\t") for ln in open(os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend", "2026-09-01.tsv"))
                if not ln.startswith("#")]
        ok("a tick under a fake HOME writes there and never touches the real ledger",
           r.returncode == 0 and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend")) and th in r.stdout + str(rows))
        ok("three records of ONE response are counted once, not three times",
           len(rows) == 1 and int(rows[0][8]) == 30)
        ok("the row carries repo, track and kind read off the cwd and the entrypoint",
           rows[0][3] == "r" and rows[0][4] == "t1" and rows[0][5] == "headless")
        ok("est_usd is the rates table applied to those tokens",
           abs(float(rows[0][11]) - usd("claude-opus-5", {"input_tokens": 200, "output_tokens": 30,
                                                          "cache_read_input_tokens": 2000})) < 1e-9)

        # a second tick with nothing new must add nothing…
        run("tick")
        before = open(os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend", "2026-09-01.tsv")).read()
        ok("a tick over a file that has not grown appends nothing", before.count("\n") == 2)

        # …and a response whose blocks straddle the boundary must not be counted twice.
        open(jl, "a").write(rec("req_c", 5))
        run("tick")
        open(jl, "a").write(rec("req_c", 5) + rec("req_d", 7))
        run("tick")
        rows = [ln.split("\t") for ln in open(os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend", "2026-09-01.tsv"))
                if not ln.startswith("#")]
        ok("a response split across two ticks is counted once (the last requestId is remembered)",
           sum(int(x[8]) for x in rows) == 30 + 5 + 7)

        # a half-written last line is left alone until it is whole
        half = '{"type":"assistant","requestId":"req_e"'
        open(jl, "a").write(half)
        run("tick")
        ok("a record still being written is left for the next tick",
           json.load(open(os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend", "offsets.json")))["files"][jl]["off"]
           == os.path.getsize(jl) - len(half))

        # truncation and disappearance both reset
        open(jl, "w").write(rec("req_f", 3))
        run("tick")
        rows = [ln.split("\t") for ln in open(os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend", "2026-09-01.tsv"))
                if not ln.startswith("#")]
        ok("a truncated transcript is re-read from the start rather than skipped",
           sum(int(x[8]) for x in rows) == 30 + 5 + 7 + 3)
        os.remove(jl)
        run("tick")
        ok("a transcript that vanished takes its offset with it",
           json.load(open(os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend", "offsets.json")))["files"] == {})

        # rows land on the day the RECORD happened, not the day the tick ran
        open(jl, "w").write(rec("req_g", 1, ts="2026-08-30T23:59:00.000Z")
                            + rec("req_h", 2, ts="2026-08-31T00:01:00.000Z"))
        run("tick")
        ok("a row is dated by the record, so the first tick backfills instead of piling history onto today",
           os.path.exists(os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend", "2026-08-30.tsv"))
           and os.path.exists(os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend", "2026-08-31.tsv")))
        offs = os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "spend", "offsets.json")
        ok("…and backfilled history is NOT 'grew this tick' — or the first tick calls every session ever a phantom",
           json.load(open(offs))["grew"] == {})
        os.remove(jl)
        run("tick")
        open(jl, "w").write(rec("req_i", 1, ts=iso(now())))
        run("tick")
        ok("a record written just now IS grew-this-tick", list(json.load(open(offs))["grew"]) == ["s1"])

        # the runs/ json cross-check: a worker's own result read back and compared
        runs = os.path.join(th, ".cc", "state", "r", "t1", "runs")
        os.makedirs(runs)
        json.dump({"total_cost_usd": 1.25, "session_id": "s1",
                   "modelUsage": {"claude-opus-5": {"costUSD": 1.25}}},
                  open(os.path.join(runs, "x.json"), "w"))
        out, today_out = run("--week").stdout, run().stdout
        ok("the cross-check weighs a worker run against the ledger's estimate for THAT session, not the whole "
           "headless column", "cross-check: 1 worker run(s) over 1 session(s) report $1.2500" in out)

        # LIKE FOR LIKE. total_cost_usd covers a run end to end and cannot be split by day, so the ledger side
        # has to be the session's whole life too. Weighing a run that finished today against today's rows alone
        # charged its whole life to its last day — the first real tick read $116 against $4.78 that way. s1 here
        # has rows on 08-30, 08-31, 09-01 and today, so the two views only agree if the window stopped counting.
        def xc(s):
            m = re.search(r"ledger estimates \$([0-9.]+) for those sessions", s)
            return float(m.group(1)) if m else None

        def shown_total(s):
            m = re.search(r"total\s+([0-9.]+)\s+<- estimate", s)
            return float(m.group(1)) if m else None

        ok("…over the session's whole life, so one run reads the same in today's view and in the week's",
           xc(out) is not None and xc(out) == xc(today_out))
        ok("…and that is more than the day's own rows, which is the like-for-like the first real tick lacked",
           shown_total(today_out) is not None and xc(today_out) > shown_total(today_out))
        ok("every view says the dollars are an estimate", "ESTIMATE" in out and "ESTIMATE" in run().stdout)
        for by in ("repo", "track", "model", "kind"):
            got = run("--by", by, "--week")
            ok(f"--by {by} groups the same rows", got.returncode == 0 and "total" in got.stdout)
        ok("an unknown --by is a usage error, not a silent empty table", run("--by", "nonesuch").returncode == 2)

        # the two phantom rules, on the pure function — no tmux server, no board, no live process
        at = datetime(2026, 9, 1, 12, 0, tzinfo=timezone.utc)
        grew = {"s1": {"cwd": cwd, "repo": "r", "track": "t1", "kind": "headless", "usd": 2.0}}
        ok("a session that grew with no window and no live board row is a phantom",
           len(phantom_rows(grew, [], set(), set(), {}, {}, at)) == 1)
        ok("…a tmux window on its directory clears it",
           phantom_rows(grew, [], {cwd}, set(), {}, {}, at) == [])
        # The box's own `box` window sits at ~/dev forever, so an ancestor pane must NOT count as watching —
        # it did once, and every ~/dev/<repo> session was silently un-phantomable.
        ok("…but a pane in a PARENT directory does not — the ~/dev window watches ~/dev, not the whole box",
           len(phantom_rows(grew, [], {os.path.dirname(cwd)}, set(), {}, {}, at)) == 1)
        ok("…while a pane that has cd'd DEEPER is still the same window, so it clears it",
           phantom_rows(grew, [], {os.path.join(cwd, "core", "bin")}, set(), {}, {}, at) == [])
        ok("…and so does a board row running WITH a loop alive behind it",
           phantom_rows(grew, [], set(), {"r/t1"}, {"r/t1": "running"}, {}, at) == []
           and len(phantom_rows(grew, [], set(), set(), {"r/t1": "running"}, {}, at)) == 1)
        ok("a board row that merely SAYS running, with no loop, is exactly what this catches",
           len(phantom_rows(grew, [], set(), {"r/other"}, {"r/t1": "running"}, {}, at)) == 1)
        old = [{"pid": 7, "etime_s": 2 * 3600, "cwd": cwd, "repo": "r", "track": "t1", "kind": "headless"}]
        got = phantom_rows({}, old, {cwd}, set(), {}, {cwd: at - timedelta(minutes=45)}, at)
        ok("a process alive over an hour whose transcript is idle 30 min is a phantom",
           len(got) == 1 and got[0]["detail"].endswith("in a window"))
        ok("…and the row says whether anyone is on it, so a left-open session reads apart from a leak",
           phantom_rows({}, old, set(), set(), {}, {cwd: at - timedelta(minutes=45)},
                        at)[0]["detail"].endswith("nobody watching"))
        ok("…and rule 2 reads the panes the same way — a parent pane is not somebody watching",
           phantom_rows({}, old, {os.path.dirname(cwd)}, set(), {}, {cwd: at - timedelta(minutes=45)},
                        at)[0]["detail"].endswith("nobody watching"))
        ok("…and one whose transcript grew five minutes ago is not",
           phantom_rows({}, old, {cwd}, set(), {}, {cwd: at - timedelta(minutes=5)}, at) == [])
        ok("a young process is never a phantom, however quiet",
           phantom_rows({}, [dict(old[0], etime_s=60)], set(), set(), {}, {}, at) == [])
        # THE TICK SAVES THE LIST — its own fixture HOME, its own transcript, timestamped now so it counts as
        # grown. Only OUR row is asserted: proc_snapshot walks the real /proc, so this box's own long-lived
        # sessions legitimately show up beside it in any run.
        th2 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ccspend-ph-")
        try:
            cwd2 = os.path.join(th2, ".cc", "worktrees", "r", "t9")
            proj2 = os.path.join(th2, ".claude", "projects", "-p2")
            os.makedirs(proj2)
            open(os.path.join(proj2, "s9session.jsonl"), "w").write(json.dumps(
                {"type": "assistant", "requestId": "req_p", "cwd": cwd2, "sessionId": "s9session",
                 "timestamp": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.000Z", time.gmtime()), "entrypoint": "sdk-cli",
                 "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5", "id": "m_p",
                             "usage": {"input_tokens": 100, "output_tokens": 10,
                                       "cache_read_input_tokens": 1000,
                                       "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}}}) + "\n")
            env2 = {**os.environ, "HOME": th2}
            r2 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "tick"], env=env2, capture_output=True, text=True)
            ph = [ln.rstrip("\n").split("\t") for ln in open(os.path.join(th2, ".cc", "state", "spend", "phantoms.tsv"))
                  if not ln.startswith("#")]
            mine = [r for r in ph if r[1] == "unwatched" and r[3] == "r" and r[4] == "t9"]
            ok("the tick saves the phantom list to phantoms.tsv, so a reader (the Home tab) shows it without "
               "re-deriving the rule from tmux, the boards and the loops",
               r2.returncode == 0 and len(mine) == 1 and "nobody watching" in mine[0][6])
            said2 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "phantoms"], env=env2, capture_output=True, text=True)
            ok("…and it is the same list `cc-spend phantoms` prints, because `grew` only moves at a tick",
               said2.returncode == 0 and "s9sessio" in said2.stdout and "unwatched" in said2.stdout)
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(th2, ignore_errors=True)
        # A SUBAGENT's transcript sits one level deeper, <project>/<session>/subagents/*.jsonl, and carries the
        # PARENT's sessionId and a real cwd — so it lands on the parent's session and the parent's lane. The
        # one-level glob this replaced never saw those files at all.
        th4 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ccspend-sub-")
        try:
            cwd4 = os.path.join(th4, ".cc", "worktrees", "r", "t4")
            proj4 = os.path.join(th4, ".claude", "projects", "-p4")
            sub4 = os.path.join(proj4, "s4", "subagents")
            os.makedirs(sub4)

            def rec4(rid, out_tok):
                return json.dumps({"type": "assistant", "requestId": rid, "cwd": cwd4, "sessionId": "s4",
                                   "timestamp": "2026-09-01T10:00:00.000Z", "entrypoint": "sdk-cli",
                                   "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5", "id": "m_" + rid,
                                               "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": out_tok,
                                                         "cache_read_input_tokens": 0,
                                                         "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}}}) + "\n"

            open(os.path.join(proj4, "s4.jsonl"), "w").write(rec4("req_top", 11))
            open(os.path.join(sub4, "agent-a1.jsonl"), "w").write(rec4("req_sub", 22))
            env4 = {**os.environ, "HOME": th4}
            day4 = os.path.join(th4, ".cc", "state", "spend", "2026-09-01.tsv")
            r4 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "tick"], env=env4, capture_output=True, text=True)
            rows4 = [ln.split("\t") for ln in open(day4) if not ln.startswith("#")]
            ok("a subagent's transcript, one level deeper, is counted — and against the parent's session and lane",
               r4.returncode == 0 and sum(int(x[8]) for x in rows4) == 33
               and {(x[1], x[3], x[4]) for x in rows4} == {("s4", "r", "t4")})
            files4 = json.load(open(os.path.join(th4, ".cc", "state", "spend", "offsets.json")))["files"]
            subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "tick"], env=env4, capture_output=True, text=True)
            ok("…once: offsets are keyed by full path, so neither file is read twice by a glob that now overlaps",
               len(files4) == 2 and len(open(day4).read().splitlines()) == len(rows4) + 1)
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(th4, ignore_errors=True)
        # …and the same record reached twice is still counted once — but a record found only in a parent is
        # never just discarded. s5 has the SAME requestId in its parent and its subagent file (neither marked
        # isSidechain — the plain cross-file dedupe). s6 has a sidechain record with NO subagents/ directory
        # at all: nothing else will ever hold it, so it counts. s7 proves the requestId match itself, not a
        # weaker stand-in for it: its subagent file arrives a TICK BEFORE the parent (so only a persisted
        # registry, not this tick's own bookkeeping, can catch the duplicate), the two copies carry different
        # cwd/model (so a coincidence of matching metadata proves nothing), and s7 ALSO has a second sidechain
        # record no subagent file anywhere carries — proving the drop reads requestIds, not "a subagents/
        # directory exists for this session".
        th5 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ccspend-dup-")
        try:
            cwd5 = os.path.join(th5, ".cc", "worktrees", "r", "t5")
            cwd7sub = os.path.join(th5, ".cc", "worktrees", "r", "t7-sub")
            proj5 = os.path.join(th5, ".claude", "projects", "-p5")
            proj6 = os.path.join(th5, ".claude", "projects", "-p6")
            proj7 = os.path.join(th5, ".claude", "projects", "-p7")
            os.makedirs(os.path.join(proj5, "s5", "subagents"))
            os.makedirs(proj6)
            os.makedirs(os.path.join(proj7, "s7", "subagents"))

            def rec5(sid, rid, out_tok, side=False, cwd=cwd5, model="claude-opus-5"):
                r = {"type": "assistant", "requestId": rid, "cwd": cwd, "sessionId": sid,
                     "timestamp": "2026-09-01T10:00:00.000Z", "entrypoint": "sdk-cli",
                     "message": {"model": model, "id": "m_" + rid,
                                 "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": out_tok,
                                           "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}}}
                if side:
                    r["isSidechain"] = True
                return json.dumps(r) + "\n"

            open(os.path.join(proj5, "s5.jsonl"), "w").write(rec5("s5", "req_dup", 11))
            open(os.path.join(proj5, "s5", "subagents", "agent-b1.jsonl"), "w").write(
                rec5("s5", "req_dup", 11, side=True))
            open(os.path.join(proj6, "s6.jsonl"), "w").write(
                rec5("s6", "req_own", 5) + rec5("s6", "req_side", 100, side=True))
            # s7's subagent file exists a tick before its parent does — a real timing this box already sees
            # (subagent writes flush as their turns finish; the parent transcript is written after).
            open(os.path.join(proj7, "s7", "subagents", "agent-c1.jsonl"), "w").write(
                rec5("s7", "req_both", 50, cwd=cwd7sub, model="claude-sonnet-5"))
            env5 = {**os.environ, "HOME": th5}
            r5 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "tick"], env=env5, capture_output=True, text=True)
            rows5 = [ln.split("\t") for ln in
                     open(os.path.join(th5, ".cc", "state", "spend", "2026-09-01.tsv")) if not ln.startswith("#")]
            out5 = {sid: sum(int(x[8]) for x in rows5 if x[1] == sid) for sid in ("s5", "s6")}
            ok("one requestId written into both a session's parent and its subagent transcript is counted "
               "once, not twice", r5.returncode == 0 and out5["s5"] == 11)
            ok("…and a sidechain record in a parent transcript with no subagents/ directory at all is counted "
               "— nothing else will ever hold that request", out5["s6"] == 105)
            # Now s7's parent arrives: req_both (isSidechain, matches the subagent file already on disk from
            # the tick above) must be dropped; req_only (isSidechain, no subagent file anywhere carries it,
            # even though s7 has one for a different request) must not be.
            open(os.path.join(proj7, "s7.jsonl"), "w").write(
                rec5("s7", "req_own7", 7) + rec5("s7", "req_both", 50, side=True)
                + rec5("s7", "req_only", 33, side=True))
            r5b = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "tick"], env=env5, capture_output=True, text=True)
            rows5b = [ln.split("\t") for ln in
                      open(os.path.join(th5, ".cc", "state", "spend", "2026-09-01.tsv")) if not ln.startswith("#")]
            out7 = sum(int(x[8]) for x in rows5b if x[1] == "s7")
            ok("…a sidechain record is dropped only when a subagent file — read in an earlier tick, under a "
               "different cwd and model — really carries its requestId: 7 + 50 + 33 = 90, not 140 from "
               "counting req_both twice nor 57 from dropping req_only too on directory presence alone",
               r5b.returncode == 0 and out7 == 90)
            # The registry doesn't just grow: a finished session's requestIds leave it once its subagents/
            # files are gone from the walk, not kept forever against every session this box has ever run.
            shutil.rmtree(os.path.join(proj7, "s7", "subagents"))
            r5c = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "tick"], env=env5, capture_output=True, text=True)
            off5 = json.load(open(os.path.join(th5, ".cc", "state", "spend", "offsets.json")))
            ok("a finished session's requestIds leave the persisted registry once its subagents/ files are "
               "gone from the walk", r5c.returncode == 0 and "s7" not in (off5.get("sub_rids") or {}))
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(th5, ignore_errors=True)
        # ---- `today`: the per-repo figure a daily ceiling reads. Its OWN fixture HOME and its own ledger
        # rows, written here rather than inherited from the tick cases above.
        th3 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ccspend-today-")
        try:
            st3 = os.path.join(th3, ".cc", "state", "spend")
            os.makedirs(st3)
            d_now, d_prev = days(2)

            def led(day, rows):
                with open(os.path.join(st3, f"{day}.tsv"), "w") as f:
                    f.write("#" + "\t".join(COLS) + "\n")
                    for repo, kind, amt in rows:
                        f.write("\t".join([f"{day}T10:00:00Z", "sX", f"/x/{repo}", repo, "t1", kind,
                                           "claude-opus-5", "0", "0", "0", "0", f"{amt:.6f}"]) + "\n")

            led(d_now, [("mine", "headless", 3.0), ("mine", "headless", 1.5),
                        ("mine", "interactive", 40.0), ("other", "headless", 7.0)])
            led(d_prev, [("mine", "headless", 99.0)])
            json.dump({"tick": iso(now())}, open(os.path.join(st3, "offsets.json"), "w"))

            def today_run(*a):
                # TZ=UTC so the child's local day is the UTC day these fixture files are named by. Without it
                # the case would pass or fail by the hour it ran at; the zone's own cases are further down.
                return subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "today", *a],
                                      env={**os.environ, "HOME": th3, "TZ": "UTC"}, capture_output=True, text=True)

            one = today_run("mine")
            ok("`today <repo>` is that repo's AUTONOMOUS spend today and nothing else — an interactive session "
               "at the same repo, another repo's worker and yesterday's rows are all left out",
               one.returncode == 0 and one.stdout.strip() == "4.5000")
            whole = today_run()
            ok("…and the table beside it shows what was left out, so the interactive $40 the ceiling figure "
               "excludes is still on the page",
               whole.returncode == 0 and "4.5000" in whole.stdout and "44.5000" in whole.stdout
               and "7.0000" in whole.stdout and "99" not in whole.stdout)
            none = today_run("never-heard-of")
            ok("a repo that has spent nothing today reads as 0, so a caller gets a number rather than an error",
               none.returncode == 0 and none.stdout.strip() == "0.0000")
            json.dump({"tick": iso(now() - timedelta(hours=2))}, open(os.path.join(st3, "offsets.json"), "w"))
            frozen = today_run("mine")
            ok("a ledger that has stopped ticking still prints the figure, and says in stderr and in its exit "
               "status that it is what the ledger knew THEN — a cap must not read a frozen number as headroom",
               frozen.stdout.strip() == "4.5000" and frozen.returncode == 1
               and "has not ticked since" in frozen.stderr)
            os.remove(os.path.join(st3, "offsets.json"))
            never = today_run("mine")
            ok("…and a box where no tick has ever run reads the same way, never as a silent $0",
               never.stdout.strip() == "4.5000" and never.returncode == 1
               and "no tick has run yet" in never.stderr)
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(th3, ignore_errors=True)
        # ---- `cache`: cache-read tokens per (session, day), off a hand-written ledger — two sessions, two
        # days, several models folded into one row each, no tick involved.
        th6 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ccspend-cache-")
        try:
            st6 = os.path.join(th6, ".cc", "state", "spend")
            os.makedirs(st6)
            d1, d0 = days(2)                   # d1 = today, d0 = yesterday

            def led6(day, rows):
                with open(os.path.join(st6, f"{day}.tsv"), "w") as f:
                    f.write("#" + "\t".join(COLS) + "\n")
                    for sid, cr, cw, i, o, amt in rows:
                        f.write("\t".join([f"{day}T10:00:00Z", sid, "/x/whatever", "repo", "t1", "headless",
                                           "claude-opus-5", str(i), str(o), str(cr), str(cw), f"{amt:.6f}"]) + "\n")

            # sessionA has two rows on d0 (two models, same session/day) that must fold into one line, not two.
            led6(d1, [("sessionA1", 4000, 0, 100, 10, 0.5), ("sessionB1", 1000, 0, 100, 10, 0.2)])
            led6(d0, [("sessionA1", 1000, 0, 100, 10, 0.1), ("sessionA1", 500, 0, 50, 5, 0.05),
                      ("sessionB1", 2000, 0, 200, 20, 0.3)])

            def cache_run(*a):
                return subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "cache", *a],   # TZ=UTC: see today_run() above
                                      env={**os.environ, "HOME": th6, "TZ": "UTC"}, capture_output=True, text=True)

            two = cache_run("--days", "2")
            s2 = two.stdout
            i4, i2k, i15, i1k = s2.find("4.0k"), s2.find("2.0k"), s2.find("1.5k"), s2.find("1.0k")
            total2 = next(ln for ln in s2.splitlines() if ln.strip().startswith("total"))
            ok("the cache report sums cache-read tokens per (session, day) across models, biggest first, with "
               "a total line",
               two.returncode == 0 and i4 >= 0 and i4 < i2k < i15 < i1k and "8.5k" in total2)
            ok("…and a session is kept apart by day, not folded across days into one row",
               "5.5k" not in s2)
            one = cache_run("--days", "1")
            total1 = next(ln for ln in one.stdout.splitlines() if ln.strip().startswith("total"))
            ok("`--days` bounds the window — `--days 1` counts only the most recent day",
               one.returncode == 0 and "5.0k" in total1
               and "2.0k" not in one.stdout and "1.5k" not in one.stdout)
            default = cache_run()
            total_def = next(ln for ln in default.stdout.splitlines() if ln.strip().startswith("total"))
            ok("…and the default window (7 days) is not silently narrowed to the one day above",
               default.returncode == 0 and "8.5k" in total_def)
            ok("a bad --days value is a usage error, not a silent default",
               cache_run("--days", "0").returncode == 2 and cache_run("--days", "nope").returncode == 2)
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(th6, ignore_errors=True)
        # ---- the box's own day. Pure first: a fixed zone forced into tz(), no clock and no fixture, so the
        # DST edges below are the same assertion in March as in September.
        global _TZ
        _tz_saved = _TZ
        try:
            _TZ = ("America/Los_Angeles", ZoneInfo("America/Los_Angeles"))
            ok("a stamp is bucketed by the day the BOX is in, not the day UTC is in — either side of the "
               "local midnight, which is mid-afternoon UTC",
               local_day("2026-09-07T06:59:59Z") == "2026-09-06"
               and local_day("2026-09-07T07:00:00Z") == "2026-09-07"
               and local_day("2026-09-08T02:00:00Z") == "2026-09-07")
            # 2026-03-08 in America/Los_Angeles is 23 hours long: it opens at 08:00Z (PST) and closes at
            # 07:00Z the next day (PDT). 2026-11-01 is 25: it opens at 07:00Z and closes at 08:00Z.
            ok("a DST day is not 24 hours and still buckets right — the 23-hour spring-forward day opens an "
               "hour late and closes an hour early",
               local_day("2026-03-08T07:30:00Z") == "2026-03-07"
               and local_day("2026-03-08T08:30:00Z") == "2026-03-08"
               and local_day("2026-03-09T06:30:00Z") == "2026-03-08"
               and local_day("2026-03-09T07:30:00Z") == "2026-03-09")
            ok("…and the 25-hour fall-back day the other way",
               local_day("2026-11-01T06:30:00Z") == "2026-10-31"
               and local_day("2026-11-02T07:30:00Z") == "2026-11-01"
               and local_day("2026-11-02T08:30:00Z") == "2026-11-02")
            ok("a local window reads the UTC ledger file either side of it, since every zone is less than a "
               "whole day from UTC", ledger_days(["2026-09-07"]) == ["2026-09-08", "2026-09-07", "2026-09-06"])
            # A day is counted from the local DATE of each row, never by adding 24 hours to a start, so the
            # spring-forward day has 23 entries and not 24 that walk into the next one.
            hours = {local_day(f"2026-03-08T{h:02d}:00:00Z") for h in range(24)} | \
                    {local_day(f"2026-03-09T{h:02d}:00:00Z") for h in range(24)}
            ok("…so no hour is ever double-counted or lost across a transition", hours == {
                "2026-03-07", "2026-03-08", "2026-03-09"} and sum(
                1 for h in range(24) if local_day(f"2026-03-08T{h:02d}:00:00Z") == "2026-03-08") == 16)
            # Where the zone comes from. $TZ first (which is how a test moves the box), then /etc/localtime,
            # which is what `timedatectl set-timezone` rewrites — nothing in this repo names a zone.
            _env0 = os.environ.get("TZ")
            os.environ["TZ"] = "Asia/Tokyo"
            from_tz = tz_name()
            os.environ.pop("TZ", None)
            from_system = tz_name()
            real = os.path.realpath("/etc/localtime")
            os.environ["TZ"] = "Mars/Olympus"
            _TZ = None
            bogus = tz()[0]
        finally:
            _TZ = _tz_saved
            os.environ.pop("TZ", None)
            if _env0 is not None:
                os.environ["TZ"] = _env0
        ok("the zone is the system's and never a config this repo owns: $TZ wins, and with no $TZ it is what "
           "/etc/localtime points at — so moving the box is `timedatectl set-timezone` and no edit here",
           from_tz == "Asia/Tokyo"
           and from_system == (real.split("/zoneinfo/")[-1] if "/zoneinfo/" in real else from_system))
        ok("…and a zone name the tz database does not know falls back to UTC under its own name rather than "
           "crashing every view, or claiming a zone it did not bucket by", bogus == "UTC")

        # ---- `--json`: the shape the dashboard was promised, end to end. Two rows an hour apart in ONE
        # UTC-named ledger file, straddling the box's local midnight.
        th8 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ccspend-json-")
        try:
            z8 = ZoneInfo("America/Los_Angeles")
            lt = datetime.now(z8).date()                       # the child's local today
            prev = lt - timedelta(days=1)

            def at_local(d, hh, mm):
                return datetime(d.year, d.month, d.day, hh, mm, tzinfo=z8).astimezone(timezone.utc)

            late, early = at_local(prev, 23, 30), at_local(lt, 0, 30)   # 23:30 yesterday, 00:30 today
            st8 = os.path.join(th8, ".cc", "state", "spend")
            os.makedirs(st8)
            utc_file = iso(late)[:10]                          # both land in the SAME UTC day file
            with open(os.path.join(st8, f"{utc_file}.tsv"), "w") as f:
                f.write("#" + "\t".join(COLS) + "\n")
                for t, amt in ((late, 2.0), (early, 5.0)):
                    f.write("\t".join([iso(t), "sJ", "/x/r", "r", "t1", "headless", "claude-opus-5",
                                      "0", "0", "0", "0", f"{amt:.6f}"]) + "\n")
            json.dump({"tick": iso(now())}, open(os.path.join(st8, "offsets.json"), "w"))

            def json_run(tzv):
                r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "--json"],
                                   env={**os.environ, "HOME": th8, "TZ": tzv}, capture_output=True, text=True)
                return r, (json.loads(r.stdout) if r.returncode == 0 else {})

            rl, jl8 = json_run("America/Los_Angeles")
            ok("`--json` names the zone it used and the boundary that produced the figures, so a reader "
               "labels what it got instead of inferring it from a version",
               rl.returncode == 0 and jl8["tz"] == "America/Los_Angeles" and jl8["day_boundary"] == "local"
               and jl8["stale"] is False and len(jl8["by_day"]) == 7
               and list(jl8["by_day"])[0] == str(lt) and str(prev) in jl8["by_day"])
            ok("…and buckets by the BOX's day: two rows an hour apart in one UTC-named ledger file land on "
               "different local days, the later one alone being today",
               jl8["by_day"][str(lt)]["usd"] == 5.0 and jl8["by_day"][str(prev)]["usd"] == 2.0
               and jl8["today"]["usd"] == 5.0 and jl8["week"]["usd"] == 7.0)
            ru, ju8 = json_run("UTC")
            ok("…while the SAME ledger read from a box in UTC puts both on one day — the zone is doing the "
               "work, so moving the box is one system setting and no edit here",
               ru.returncode == 0 and ju8["tz"] == "UTC" and ju8["by_day"][utc_file]["usd"] == 7.0)
            ok("the ledger itself never moved: both rows are in ONE UTC-named file, still stamped in UTC — "
               "it is the view that re-buckets, so reconciliation still compares like with like",
               utc_file == iso(early)[:10] and os.path.exists(os.path.join(st8, f"{utc_file}.tsv"))
               and all(ln.split("\t")[0].endswith("Z") for ln in
                       open(os.path.join(st8, f"{utc_file}.tsv")) if not ln.startswith("#")))
            json.dump({"tick": iso(now() - timedelta(hours=2))}, open(os.path.join(st8, "offsets.json"), "w"))
            rs, js8 = json_run("America/Los_Angeles")
            ok("a frozen ledger is a FIELD in the payload and not a non-zero exit — a reader that raises on "
               "the status would throw away a complete answer over something it can read",
               rs.returncode == 0 and js8["stale"] is True and js8["today"]["usd"] == 5.0)
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(th8, ignore_errors=True)
        # ---- a steward run's transcript lands on its own `<repo>/steward` line in `--by track`, apart from a
        # planning session's `<repo>/session` line — the place() case above, end to end through a real tick.
        th7 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ccspend-stew-")
        try:
            cwd_st = os.path.join(th7, ".cc", "steward", "myrepo")
            cwd_sess = os.path.join(th7, "dev", "myrepo")
            cwd_ctl = os.path.join(th7, "dev", "otherdayrepo")
            proj_st = os.path.join(th7, ".claude", "projects", "-stew-proj")
            proj_sess = os.path.join(th7, ".claude", "projects", "-sess-proj")
            proj_ctl = os.path.join(th7, ".claude", "projects", "-ctl-proj")
            os.makedirs(proj_st)
            os.makedirs(proj_sess)
            os.makedirs(proj_ctl)

            # The child is put in a zone whose date is DELIBERATELY not UTC's, so the skew that took this case
            # red (21:53 PDT, 2026-09-08: the row landed on a local day the view does not cover) is reproduced
            # at whatever hour the suite runs at, not only when this box is in that evening itself. UTC-10
            # before 10:00Z puts the child on the previous local date, UTC+13 from 11:00Z on the next one; both
            # are fixed offsets, so no DST hour is ambiguous or skipped. Stamping the rows the way this fixture
            # used to — UTC's date at 10:00Z — then puts them off the child's today and turns the two
            # assertions below red, which is what makes them a check. (Not in the 10:00Z hour: there the old
            # stamp's own hour is the hour the run is in, and no zone separates the two dates.)
            t0 = now()                                   # ONE clock read: the zone, the stamps and the control
            west = t0.hour < 10                          # day cannot then be picked off opposite sides of 10:00Z
            tz7 = "Etc/GMT+10" if west else "Etc/GMT-13"
            lday = t0.astimezone(ZoneInfo(tz7)).date()   # the day the child is in, which the view will read
            # Stamped NOW, so the two rows are in the child's local today whatever day that is, and read back
            # through the DEFAULT view, which is that one local date — `--week` was seven of them and hid the
            # skew. The control is stamped on `lday` read as a UTC day, half a day past whichever edge the
            # offset crosses: its LOCAL date is a day off today, so the view must not show it, while its UTC
            # day is `lday` itself — inside ledger_days(), so the file really is opened and only the local-date
            # filter keeps the row out. Bucket the view on the UTC day, or drop that filter, and it shows up.
            ts7 = iso(t0)
            ts_ctl = f"{lday}T00:30:00Z" if west else f"{lday}T23:30:00Z"

            def rec7(rid, sid, cwdv, ts=None):
                return json.dumps({"type": "assistant", "requestId": rid, "timestamp": ts or ts7,
                                   "cwd": cwdv, "sessionId": sid, "entrypoint": "sdk-cli",
                                   "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5", "id": "m_" + rid,
                                               "usage": {"input_tokens": 100, "output_tokens": 10,
                                                         "cache_read_input_tokens": 1000,
                                                         "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}}}) + "\n"

            open(os.path.join(proj_st, "sstewa1.jsonl"), "w").write(rec7("req_stew", "sstewa1", cwd_st))
            open(os.path.join(proj_sess, "ssessi1.jsonl"), "w").write(rec7("req_sess", "ssessi1", cwd_sess))
            open(os.path.join(proj_ctl, "sctlrw1.jsonl"), "w").write(
                rec7("req_ctl", "sctlrw1", cwd_ctl, ts_ctl))
            env7 = {**os.environ, "HOME": th7, "TZ": tz7}
            subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "tick"], env=env7, capture_output=True, text=True)
            by_track = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "--by", "track"], env=env7,
                                      capture_output=True, text=True)
            ok("a steward-placed transcript lands on its own `myrepo/steward` line",
               by_track.returncode == 0 and "myrepo/steward" in by_track.stdout)
            ok("…while a session one still lands on `myrepo/session`, and the two are not folded into one row",
               "myrepo/session" in by_track.stdout and by_track.stdout.count("myrepo/") == 2)
            ok("…and the day they are read back on is the CHILD's own, not UTC's: a control row sitting in a "
               "ledger file this view does open, a local day off the child's today, stays out of the table — "
               "bucket on the UTC day instead, or drop the local-date filter, and it is the row that shows",
               "otherdayrepo" not in by_track.stdout)
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(th7, ignore_errors=True)
        ok("`phantoms` before any tick says so and exits 1 rather than printing an empty all-clear",
           subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "phantoms"],
                          env={**os.environ, "HOME": tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ccspend-empty-")},
                          capture_output=True, text=True).returncode == 1)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(th, ignore_errors=True)

    # A PIPE PLANTED WHERE A BOARD SHOULD BE (review of #224): board_states reads through cc-board's opener, so it is skipped
    # and the 5-minute timer is never hung by a member
    def bounded(fn, secs=10):   # a read that hangs on the pipe (a plain open would, for good) fails THIS case, not the suite
        import threading
        box = []
        def go():
            try: box.append(fn())
            except Exception as e: box.append(e)
        t = threading.Thread(target=go, daemon=True); t.start(); t.join(secs)
        return box[0] if box else "hung"
    global BOARDS
    _b, BOARDS = BOARDS, tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-spend-pipe-")
    _mb = None
    try:
        os.mkfifo(os.path.join(BOARDS, "pipe.json"))
        json.dump({"repo": "real", "tracks": {"t": {"status": "running"}}}, open(os.path.join(BOARDS, "real.json"), "w"))
        # …AND A LINK WITH NOTHING AT THE END OF IT reads as None, not as a board: a member workspace whose board file is
        # gone while ~/.cc/boards/<h>.json still links to it, or a board unlinked between the glob above and this read.
        # `.get` on that None — it sits outside the try — took every 5-minute run of this tool down with an AttributeError
        # (review of #224). cc-board only follows such a link to ~/.cc/members/<h>/board/<h>.json, so its own BOARDS has to
        # point at this fixture for the rule to read here rather than at the box's real one.
        board_read(os.path.join(BOARDS, "real.json"))                 # loads the module, so .mod is there to point
        _mb, board_read.mod.BOARDS = board_read.mod.BOARDS, BOARDS
        os.symlink(os.path.join(BOARDS, os.pardir, "members", "gone", "board", "gone.json"), os.path.join(BOARDS, "gone.json"))
        ok("a pipe planted where a board should be, and a link with no board at the end of it, are both skipped — no hang, "
           "no crash — and the real board beside them is read",
           bounded(board_states) == {"real/t": "running"})
    finally:
        if _mb is not None: board_read.mod.BOARDS = _mb
        shutil.rmtree(BOARDS, ignore_errors=True); BOARDS = _b
    print(f"cc-spend selfcheck: {n[0]} failed")
    return 1 if n[0] else 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- cli

USAGE = ("usage: cc-spend [--by repo|track|model|kind] [--week] | today [<repo>] | cache [--days N] "
         "| --json | phantoms | tick | selfcheck")


def bail():
    print(USAGE, file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)


def main(argv):
    if argv and argv[0] == "today":                      # the only subcommand that takes an argument
        if len(argv) > 2 or (len(argv) == 2 and argv[1].startswith("-")):
            bail()
        return cmd_today(argv[1] if len(argv) == 2 else None)
    if argv and argv[0] == "cache":
        rest = argv[1:]
        n = 7
        if rest:
            if len(rest) != 2 or rest[0] != "--days":
                bail()
            try:
                n = int(rest[1])
            except ValueError:
                bail()
            if n < 1:
                bail()
        return cmd_cache(n)
    if argv and argv[0] in ("tick", "phantoms", "selfcheck", "--json"):
        if len(argv) > 1:                                # --json is the whole command; it takes no other flag
            bail()
        return {"tick": tick, "phantoms": phantoms, "selfcheck": selfcheck, "--json": cmd_json}[argv[0]]()
    by, window = "session", local_days(1)
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a == "--week":
            window = local_days(7)
        elif a == "--by" and i + 1 < len(argv):
            by = argv[i + 1]
            i += 1
            if by not in ("repo", "track", "model", "kind", "session"):
                bail()
        elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__.strip())
            return 0
        else:
            bail()
        i += 1
    return view(by, window)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except BrokenPipeError:
        os._exit(0)
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
